{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>YHWH will fight for you.</b> It is known that the attribute of judgment is what wages war and dries up the sea, and the reason he mentioned the special Name is to indicate that even in this mighty miracle, in the destruction of the Egyptians, the attribute of mercy was partnered with the attribute of judgment. And even though it appears that the attribute of judgment was stretched out against them to destroy them with utter destruction, as it is written, ״not one of them remained,״ we find there the partnership of the attribute of mercy in that they were given burial, as it is written, ״נטית ימינך תבלעמו ארץ״ — You stretched out Your right hand, the earth swallowed them — and the right hand is the attribute of kindness. And so we find for the future, in the matter of Gog, where it is written (Zechariah 14:3): ״ויצא ה׳ ונלחם בגוים ההם״, and there is no doubt that this is said of the attribute of judgment; yet even so, we find there the partnership of mercy, namely that they will merit burial, for thus it is written (Ezekiel 39:11): ״ביום ההוא אתן לגוג מקום שם קבר״." "<b>And the explanation of the derashah:</b> ״YHWH will fight for you״ — for since they saw in the plague of the firstborn that the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself and in His glory was the one striking, and still they pursue after you, their intention is not to come against you but against Him, blessed be He; and since this is so, the battle is His, and you have nothing to do but to be silent. Or one may say <b>you shall hold your peace</b> — you will need to hold your peace, so that the attribute of judgment not turn against you, because you were sinners like them, idolaters and growers of sidelocks."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The phrase לא נשאר בהם עד אחד is an unattributed biblical paraphrase (cf. Exodus 14:28); retained as quoted without adding a citation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:59.207751437Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}